(Bug #91: an out-of-range read yields the number 0 and sets the error flag
 (LANGUAGE.md, Lists: "Out-of-bounds access sets an error flag and returns
 0"). Where the miss is not provable - an Append-grown list, a variable
 index - that 0 used to reach a `text` destination and be dereferenced as a
 pointer on the first read, so the program segfaulted. A text destination
 takes the empty text instead, which is what an unwritten `text` slot has
 held since #25. The error flag is untouched, so `On error` still fires.)

a list called grown is ["a", "b"].
Append "c" to grown.

(The working neighbour: an in-range read is unchanged.)
a text called second is element 2 of grown.
Print "in range: {second}".

(The headline: a literal index past the end.)
a text called 'past the end' is element 5 of grown.
Print "past the end: [{'past the end'}]".

(A variable index no static proof can reach.)
a number called wanted is 9.
a text called 'by index' is element wanted of grown.
Print "by index: [{'by index'}]".

(An index below the first element is the same miss.)
a number called 'before the first' is 0.
a text called below is element 'before the first' of grown.
Print "below: [{below}]".

(A miss with no destination at all still prints as the empty text.)
Print "direct: [" without newline.
Print element 5 of grown without newline.
Print "]".

(Reassignment is the declaration's twin.)
a text called seeded is "seed".
Set seeded to element 5 of grown.
Print "reassigned: [{seeded}]".

(An empty list has no first and no last.)
a list called 'no letters' is [].
(#72 proves a literal empty list has no first, and refuses a text
 destination outright - its compile_fail case 178. #91 owns the other half:
 the miss the compiler CANNOT prove. This Append never runs, so the list is
 still empty, but an Append reaches it and the proof is withheld.)
a number called 'never grows' is 0.
While 'never grows' is less than 0,
    Append "x" to 'no letters',
    Set 'never grows' to 'never grows' add 1.
a text called 'first of nothing' is 'no letters''s first.
a text called 'last of nothing' is 'no letters''s last.
Print "first of empty: [{'first of nothing'}]".
Print "last of empty: [{'last of nothing'}]".

(The empty text is a real text, so every later read of the slot works.)
a list called collected is [].
Append 'past the end' to collected.
Print "appended: {collected}".

(A number destination still takes the manual's own 0, unchanged.)
a list called counts is [1, 2].
Append 3 to counts.
a number called 'missing count' is element 5 of counts.
Print "number destination: {'missing count'}".

(And the miss is still an error, which is the whole point of yielding a
 value rather than crashing.)
a text called 'last miss' is element 5 of grown.
On error Print "the error flag was set".
